Today's entry is **Robinhood for pet tarantulas**. It took one reroll because the first draw was already in history. The product is called **Molt**: a commission-free brokerage where orders only execute when the spider moves, which it rarely does.

- **Landing page:** a soft clay look on a beige "substrate" background, with Calistoga and Inter fonts and red-knee orange used only on the main buttons and the top price tier. The hero is a phone mockup hanging from a silk thread. Each feature card shows an eight-leg meter.
- **Demo:** Gertrude's trading screen. You can switch chart ranges, open an order sheet by tapping a holding, and place an order that waits until all eight legs have moved. "Nudge enclosure" moves a few legs at random. There's also a Burrow mode toggle that blurs the balances, and a molt calendar tab.
- **Images:** three tarantula headshots for the testimonials (rose hair, Brazilian black, red-knee). All three passed on the first try. I skipped a hero image and demo images on purpose.
- **Gallery:** rebuilt with the new entry first, and the count now reads 120. The rebuild also changed an escaped apostrophe to a plain one in two older taglines; the pages display the same.
- **History and log:** today's entry is added to `state/history.json` and the run summary is appended to `state/runs/2026-10-11.log`. I appended rather than overwrote because the wrapper was already writing to that log.
- **Validation:** the metadata build and `validate-entry.sh` both passed, and passed again after the visual-check edits.
- **Visual check:** I took two screenshots and one re-shot, and made two fixes. The bottom headline on the landing page was showing in the wrong font. On the demo, a thin strip of the empty pop-up message showed at the top of the screen; I hid it, along with the closed order sheet. Side effect: the order sheet now disappears instantly when closed instead of sliding down.

